    "reasoning": "With letters I, J, J, L, T on my rack, I need to find a valid move that doesn't lengthen an already-completed sequence, so I'm looking for a spot where I can cross perpendicular to existing tiles instead of extending along their axis.\n\nLooking at the grid, the cell holding R at the origin starts both an axis-1 line and an axis-3 line that look complete, but the axis-0 direction through that same point seems entirely open with no neighboring tiles in other axes. That means I could extend along axis-0 starting from an adjacent empty cell without touching any finished line, since the surrounding cells at higher x-values are all unoccupied.\n\nOnly one bonus point reachable at x=2, so I'll settle for a sequence of R plus letters I, J, J, L, T with the higher-value letter at that position. Totaling it up: letters sum to 7 plus R's 1 gives 8, and the x=2 bonus adds 4 more, for 12 total. I'm considering whether placing R elsewhere changes anything, but the bonus position stays fixed either way.\n\nChecking axis 4 gives a similar mismatch, and other anchor points don't beat the 12 vs 14 tradeoff meaningfully, so I'll settle for the valid 12 configuration. Now I need to pick the actual letters for the word—starting with R, then choosing x1 through x5 (with x2 being I or L)—while avoiding forbidden trigrams and making sure the axis-0 move doesn't overlap any existing complete sequence through the origin, which it doesn't since origin only belongs to axis1 and axis3 lines.\n\nI'm testing candidate orderings of I, J, J, L, T after R, checking each trigram against the forbidden list, and ruling out options like R-T-L-J and R-J-L due to banned triples, landing on R-J-I as a workable start before checking what follows.\n\nChecking another path with L before I also gives a valid sequence, scoring the same 12 total. So I'll settle on the sequence R, J, I, T, J, L with axis 0, giving score 1+1+6+1+1+2=12.\n\n",
